Bottom line: The group discussed using Okta as a central identity and governance layer for AI agents, while continuing to use existing tools such as CrowdStrike, JAMF, SALT, SailPoint and CyberArk where they fit. The immediate focus is discovery: establish what agents and MCP servers exist before deciding which resources to register, which access patterns to standardize, and which traffic should pass through an agent gateway. Those implementation choices were not finalized.
1. What the team can see today
• Agents on builder platforms: Okta described direct connections to platforms including AWS Bedrock, Copilot Studio and Salesforce AgentForce. Its connector would read the agents exposed by each platform’s API. The group referred to these as known agents.
• Locally running agents and MCP servers: These require endpoint or other telemetry rather than a builder-platform connection. CrowdStrike Guardian was described as providing visibility into activity on laptops, including Kiro and Kiro MCP servers. The team is in the middle of a migration from AIDR to CrowdStrike Guardian. JAMF AI Governance provides some Mac-specific visibility.
• Cloud and API visibility: SALT Security is already used as an API security tool, with an agent-related capability for cloud activity. A SALT–GitHub integration is starting; its described scope in this discussion was GitHub activity hitting MCP servers in the environment. How its findings should be reconciled with Okta’s direct platform discovery remains a question.
• Limits of discovery: Coverage depends on the platform API or telemetry source being available. For example, endpoint discovery cannot be assumed to find activity on a device where its sensor is absent or disabled. The group wants to compare findings across tools rather than assume any one source is complete.
2. What Okta proposed after discovery
Okta described registering each discovered agent as an identity, assigning it an owner, and defining who can use it, what resources it can reach and what it is allowed to do. Its proposed agent gateway would sit in the runtime path to MCP resources, where tools and access could be scoped. Okta also described access reviews and certifications, while acknowledging the preference expressed in the meeting to keep governance work in the existing SailPoint platform where appropriate. CyberArk was similarly discussed as the preferred existing location for static credentials, rather than assuming Okta’s credential vault should replace it.
A key example was a shared agent: access to a resource should reflect both the agent’s permissions and those of the person using it. Okta called this effective permissions. In the example discussed, sharing an agent with another employee should not, by itself, give that employee the creator’s access to an enterprise resource. The speakers distinguished this resource-access control from a separate question: whether the agent’s own platform shares conversation history when the agent is shared.
Okta also described logging which agent accessed a resource on behalf of which user, so activity would not appear solely as use of a shared API key or solely as the human user. This was presented as part of the value of routing and governing the access through Okta.
3. Gateway and “kill switch” qualifications
This was a major point of discussion because discovery is not the same as runtime control. Okta’s gateway-dependent controls require the relevant agent-to-resource access to go through its gateway. The group raised the practical cost of changing agents that teams have already built elsewhere, as well as concerns about adding a hop to automated workflows. The proposal was to make risk-based decisions about which use cases need that path, rather than treating gateway adoption as already settled.
The current and planned token controls were distinguished:
• Today: manual deactivation stops new token issuance.
• Roadmap: revocation of already issued, active tokens, where the relevant platform supports it, alongside broader threat-response capabilities.
• Still to define: which anomalous behaviours should trigger which response—for example, revoking a token or terminating a session.
CrowdStrike’s kill-switch capability was also discussed, but the group did not establish it as a substitute for revoking tokens issued for access to MCP resources. They planned to validate the specifics of its coverage and gateway dependency.
4. Governance and architecture questions still open
• Agent lifecycle: A speaker said there does not yet appear to be an established agent equivalent of joiner/mover/leaver processes. Work with Deloitte on MCP and AI standards, alongside IAM controls, was identified as a place to develop those processes.
• Homegrown agents: An AI governance board and approved-tool list exist, but participants questioned whether people building their own agents—particularly in AWS—go through a consistent review. This was raised as a concern, not confirmed as the practice for every team.
• Overlapping gateways and controls: The group discussed Okta’s gateway alongside CrowdStrike Guardian’s gateway and AWS gateway options. They have not decided which should secure particular agent-to-resource paths, or how to coordinate signals and controls across products.
• Agents built outside the proposed Okta pattern: Participants want to identify what has already been built in AWS or other environments, what visibility and controls they currently have, and what would have to change to bring selected use cases into the agreed governance pattern. The potential risks and trade-offs may need to be taken to leadership.
• Developer workflows: The group wants a workable pattern for teams using highly automated CI/CD pipelines, rather than designing controls that do not accommodate how they create connections and agents.
5. Product capabilities and roadmap shown
The Okta team highlighted local agent and MCP-server discovery using the CrowdStrike Falcon integration, plus planned expansion of discovery options through Okta Verify and other endpoint integrations. They also showed or discussed a configuration view of agents, users and connected resources; cross-tenant access; consumption of third-party identity-provider tokens such as Entra tokens; CIMD support for agent authentication; SPIFFE support for workload identities; and a view of the potential resources an agent can reach. These should not all be treated as generally available today—the presentation mixed existing capabilities with roadmap items.
One implementation concern was endpoint overhead. The group clarified that the proposed CrowdStrike–Okta integration is through CrowdStrike’s API, not direct communication between the Falcon and Okta Verify agents on the laptop. They still identified integration overhead and discovery coverage as things to test, rather than assuming the impact is known.
